            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tBy 1966, Gillett G. Griffin (1928-2016), Princeton, NJ [1]; 1968, sold to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] Griffin lent to the Museum in 1966 (L.1966.173).\n\t<br />\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"The fine striations indicating this figure’s hair and the large pendant covering his chest appear frequently on Beni Zaa offering vessels. Made in sets of four to accompany single, larger objects, they are known as companion figures. The symbolism associates the figure depicted with maize agriculture, suggesting it is a maize deity. The long, delineated hair suggests the silk of a corncob, while the pectoral is an abstract representation of a milpa, or maize field, in its rectilinear shape. Each corner of the pectoral is adorned with representations of maize plants while a fifth maize motif extends downward at the center of the pendant. Together, the plants center the field by alluding to the four intercardinal directions and the center. The placement of four such figures accompanying a fifth primary figure in a tomb incorporates the same directional symbolism, effectively centering the primary vessel.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T6-3_CLA_FA.pdf  - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
